UPDATE challenge.challenges SET quiz_display_count = NULL WHERE id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_121');
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_121'), 'The spec says: "No duplicates. At most 50 bookmarks per user." The developer wrote five acceptance tests before prompting the agent, and all of them pass on the agent''s code:

  ✓ add then list returns the lesson
  ✓ list is most recent first
  ✓ adding the same lesson twice returns "already_bookmarked"
  ✓ the 51st bookmark returns "limit_reached"
  ✓ an unknown lesson returns "lesson_not_found"

The agent''s add():

add(userId: string, lessonId: string, at: number): AddResult {
  if (!this.lessons.has(lessonId)) return "lesson_not_found";
  const bookmarks = this.byUser.get(userId) ?? [];
  if (bookmarks.length >= 50) return "limit_reached";
  if (bookmarks.some((b) => b.lessonId === lessonId)) return "already_bookmarked";
  bookmarks.push({ lessonId, at });
  this.byUser.set(userId, bookmarks);
  return "added";
}

Statement: "Because all five acceptance tests pass, a user who has 50 bookmarks and taps Bookmark on a lesson that is already in the list gets \"already_bookmarked\"."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The code checks the limit before it checks for a duplicate, so a user at 50 bookmarks gets "limit_reached" for a lesson they already saved, and the app tells them to delete bookmarks to save something that is already saved. None of the five tests puts both rules in play at once: the duplicate test runs with one bookmark, the limit test with 50 different lessons. Tests prove exactly the cases they contain. The fix is a sixth acceptance test for the interaction (50 bookmarks, re-add one of them) and a spec that states the order of the checks. The misconception is that "every spec rule has a passing test" means "the spec is implemented": rules interact, and each interaction you care about needs its own case.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
