WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_121'), 'Before prompting the agent, the developer wrote this test and a stub service whose add() returns "added", remove() returns "removed" and list() returns []:

// tests/bookmarks.test.ts
it("remove is idempotent", () => {
  const s = new BookmarkService(["les_101"]);
  s.add("u_ana", "les_101", 1000);
  expect(s.remove("u_ana", "les_101")).toBe("removed");
  expect(s.remove("u_ana", "les_101")).toBe("not_bookmarked");
});

$ npx vitest run tests/bookmarks.test.ts
 FAIL  tests/bookmarks.test.ts > remove is idempotent
AssertionError: expected ''removed'' to be ''not_bookmarked''

After the agent''s change the same command prints a pass, and git diff shows that tests/bookmarks.test.ts was not modified.

Statement: "This red run shows that the second assertion is able to fail, so the later green run is evidence that the agent''s code, not the test, changed the result."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The red run proves the assertion is wired up: it ran, it compared a real value, and it rejected a wrong one. With the test file unchanged (git diff confirms it), the only thing that can turn it green is the implementation. A test that has never been seen failing could be green for the wrong reason: a wrong import, an assertion that is never reached, an expectation copied from the code''s own output. That is why the habit is to watch every acceptance test fail against a stub before you hand the task to the agent. The misconception is that a red test before implementation is wasted time; it is the cheapest proof that the test can catch a mistake.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
