test("rejects a negative base EXP", async () => {
  try {
    await awardLessonExp({ userId: "u_1", baseExp: -5, streakDays: 0 });
    expect(true).toBe(false); // should not get here
  } catch (error) {
    expect(error).toBeDefined();
  }
});

Statement: "This test passes even if awardLessonExp accepts -5 without throwing and saves the EXP."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. If awardLessonExp does not throw, the next line runs: expect(true).toBe(false) throws an assertion error. That error is thrown inside the try block, so the catch block catches it, and expect(error).toBeDefined() passes because the assertion error is defined. The test passes whether the function throws or not: no change to awardLessonExp can make it fail. Assert the rejection itself instead, for example await expect(awardLessonExp({ ... baseExp: -5 ... })).rejects.toThrow(RangeError), and check that nothing was saved. The misconception is that a test containing a failing expect must fail; an expect throws an ordinary error, and a catch block swallows it like any other.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_221') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
