    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_231'), '// src/lib/streaks.ts, written by an AI assistant
const dayOf = (ts: string) => new Date(ts).toISOString().slice(0, 10);

Linh lives in Ho Chi Minh City (UTC+07:00). The API returns two of her completions in UTC:

"2026-10-20T15:00:00Z"   (22:00 on 20 October, her time)
"2026-10-20T23:30:00Z"   (06:30 on 21 October, her time)

Statement: "dayOf() puts these two completions on two consecutive days, so the streak code counts 2 days for them."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. toISOString() always formats the instant in UTC, so both completions become "2026-10-20": the same day, counted once. For Linh they are on 20 and 21 October, two days of a streak. Whatever the server''s or the test machine''s time zone is, this code computes UTC calendar days, which match local days only for learners at UTC+00:00 - and the demo data and the developer''s own test were exactly that. The fix is to shift the instant by the learner''s offset before taking the date (or to use the learner''s time zone explicitly). The misconception is that an ISO string with a date in it is "the date" of the event; a calendar day only exists relative to a time zone.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_231') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
