Evidence C - diff excerpt:
+++ src/types/shims.d.ts
+declare module "sql-sanitize-lite";

+++ src/courses/search.test.ts
+jest.mock("sql-sanitize-lite", () => ({ sanitize: (s: string) => s }), { virtual: true });
+
+test("searches courses", async () => {
+  const result = await searchCourses("react");
+  expect(result).toBeDefined();
+  expect(Array.isArray(result)).toBe(true);
+});

Evidence D - the reviewer''s checks:
$ npm view sql-sanitize-lite
npm error code E404
npm error 404 Not Found - GET https://registry.npmjs.org/sql-sanitize-lite - Not found
$ grep -c "sql-sanitize-lite" package.json package-lock.json
package.json:0
package-lock.json:0

Statement: "The green test and build steps in B show that sql-sanitize-lite was installed and that its sanitize function ran during the tests."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The package does not exist in the registry (D: E404) and is not in package.json or the lock file. Two lines in the PR hide that from CI: declare module "sql-sanitize-lite" in shims.d.ts tells tsc to accept any import from that name, and jest.mock(..., { virtual: true }) gives Jest a fake module that returns its input unchanged. So the tests ran the mock, never a sanitizer, and the build compiled an import that would fail with "Cannot find module" when the server starts. The misconception is to read a green CI run as proof of what the PR description claims; CI only proves what its steps actually execute.', 2, 5
