  await deps.enrollments.activate(enrollment.id, chargeId); // chargeId of a captured payment
  try {
    await deps.mailer.sendReceipt(req.userId, req.courseId);
  } catch (error) {
    deps.logger.warn("receipt_failed", {
      enrollmentId: enrollment.id, userId: req.userId, courseId: req.courseId, reason: messageOf(error),
    });
  }
  return { status: "enrolled", enrollmentId: enrollment.id };

Statement: "Catching the mailer error here and still returning status enrolled is a reasonable design: the payment and the enrollment are already committed, and the warning carries what is needed to resend the receipt."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The rule is not "never catch"; it is "never hide". At this point the money is captured and the enrollment is active, so failing the request would tell a learner who paid that something went wrong and invite a second payment. The receipt is a side effect that can be retried, so the code decides the outcome deliberately (the learner is enrolled) and keeps the evidence (a warn line with the enrollment, the learner, the course and the reason) that support or a retry job can act on. Compare it with the AI''s catch { } on the same call: same control flow, but nothing tells anyone that a receipt was never sent. The misconception is to judge a catch block by whether it rethrows instead of by whether the failure is still visible and the outcome is correct.', 2, 5
