WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_331'), 'package.json in a PR, before and after:
before: "dependencies": { "express": "^4.21.2", "pg": "^8.13.1", "zod": "^3.23.8" },
        "devDependencies": { "jest": "^29.7.0", "lodash": "^4.17.21" }
after:  "dependencies": { "express": "^4.21.2", "pg": "^8.13.1", "zod": "^3.24.1",
                          "lodash": "^4.17.21", "sql-sanitize-lite": "^1.4.2" },
        "devDependencies": { "jest": "^29.7.0" }

policy.knownDependencies = ["express", "pg", "zod", "jest"]
The dependency check reports every name that is in the after manifest (dependencies or devDependencies), was in neither section before, and is not in knownDependencies.

Statement: "The dependency check reports sql-sanitize-lite and nothing else for this diff."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. zod only changed its version range, so it is not new. lodash moved from devDependencies to dependencies; it was already in the project, so it is not new either, even though it is not on the allowlist. sql-sanitize-lite is in neither section before and not on the allowlist, so it is the one finding - the package the agent invented in lesson 3.1, this time added to package.json. The rule targets the supply-chain question "what code is entering the project that nobody has vetted?"; version bumps and moves are reviewed differently (changelog, lock file diff). The misconception is that any change to package.json is a new dependency, which makes the check so noisy that people stop reading it.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aad_331') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
